SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding the Situation in Ukraine","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00008.html","claim":"Kishida said Russia's recognition of the breakaway regions was unacceptable, an infringement of Ukraine's sovereignty and territorial integrity, and that Japan would coordinate responses, including sanctions, with the G7 and the international community.","locator":"Opening answer on Japan's response to Russia's recognition of the regions in Ukraine","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official PM statement made the same day the crisis escalated, with direct attribution to Kishida.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate pre-invasion escalation in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida-led diplomatic response to a major interstate crisis.","contrary_evidence":["This is reactive diplomacy, not proof of broader peace-building success."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding the Situation in Ukraine","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00013.html","claim":"Kishida called Russia's invasion of Ukraine an attempt to unilaterally change the status quo by force and said Japan had already urged Russia to resolve the situation through diplomacy rather than force.","locator":"Opening statement on the invasion and Japan's diplomatic efforts","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PM statement contemporaneous with the invasion and Japan's first sanctions response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate wartime crisis response in February 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida condemnation of aggression and preference for diplomacy.","contrary_evidence":["The statement condemns aggression but does not itself end the war or prevent escalation."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sanction Measures following the launch of military actions by Russia in Ukraine (Statement by Foreign Minister HAYASHI Yoshimasa)","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Japan","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.mofa.go.jp/press/release/press6e_000371.html","claim":"Japan described Russia's actions as a serious violation of international law and the UN Charter and announced sanctions including visa suspensions, asset freezes, and export controls on military-related and dual-use goods.","locator":"Items 2-5 of the statement","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official foreign-ministry sanctions notice that records Japan's state response and legal framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate 2022 Ukraine war response","ruler_attribution":"Implemented under Kishida's government, though issued by the foreign minister rather than Kishida personally.","contrary_evidence":["Sanctions are coercive and align Japan against Russia, but the target is the aggressor state rather than a war of choice by Japan."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.4","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding His Attendance at the G7 Summit and NATO Summit","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-06-25","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202206/_00011.html","claim":"Kishida said the G7 would discuss responses to Russian aggression against Ukraine and demonstrate G7 unity, and that he would attend the NATO Summit as the first Japanese prime minister ever to do so in order to raise Japan's cooperation with NATO to a new stage.","locator":"Opening remarks on the G7 and NATO summits","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official pre-summit press conference directly sets out Kishida's agenda and diplomatic choice.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 alliance and multilateral diplomacy","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida foreign-policy initiative.","contrary_evidence":["Closer alignment with NATO is stabilizing for allies but can be read by rivals as bloc consolidation."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Contribution to EL PAÍS by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio on the Occasion of His Visit to Spain for the NATO Summit","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202206/_00013.html","claim":"Kishida wrote that Russian aggression against Ukraine had degraded the international order, that 'Ukraine today may be East Asia tomorrow,' and that Japan had fundamentally changed its diplomacy toward Russia while levying strong sanctions and providing strong assistance to Ukraine.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and the 'Ukraine today may be East Asia tomorrow' passage","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official article in the PM's own words, with direct policy framing and explicit regional analogy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"NATO summit visit in June 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida justification for Japan's international-security posture.","contrary_evidence":["The piece mixes warning language with statecraft; it does not by itself prove a proportional or de-escalatory end-state."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Outcome of Prime Minister Kishida's Attendance at the NATO Summit Meeting","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/diplomatic/202206/_00006.html","claim":"The PMO said Kishida attended NATO at NATO's invitation, the first Japanese prime minister ever to participate, and that discussions focused on future cooperation in light of Russian aggression against Ukraine and the severe security situation in the Indo-Pacific.","locator":"Item 1 of the meeting overview","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official summit outcome memo tied to a specific date and forum.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 NATO summit cycle","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida diplomatic choice to broaden security cooperation.","contrary_evidence":["This is cooperative security diplomacy, not direct war conduct; some observers may see it as bloc hardening rather than pure peace-making."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Keynote Address by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio at the IISS Shangri-La Dialogue","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202206/_00002.html","claim":"Kishida said the rule of law, peaceful dispute resolution, non-use of force, and respect for sovereignty are the foundation of the international order and that one cannot be allowed to unilaterally change rules by force.","locator":"Section 2-1, 'Maintaining and Strengthening the Rules-based Free and Open International Order'","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official policy speech, with direct doctrinal language on lawful security and anti-aggression norms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Early-to-mid 2022 strategic framing","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida framing of Japan's lawful-security doctrine.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is normative and does not itself show implementation or costs."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding North Korea's Launch of a Ballistic Missile and Prime Minister Kishida's Thoughts on the First Year of His Administration","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-10-04","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202210/_00002.html","claim":"After a North Korean ballistic missile launch, Kishida called the launch outrageous and unacceptable, said Japan would respond immediately, and instructed his government to confirm public safety and maintain the highest readiness while coordinating closely with the United States.","locator":"Opening remarks on North Korea's missile launch","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official crisis-response statement with immediate operational instructions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"October 2022 missile crisis","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida crisis management and alliance coordination.","contrary_evidence":["High readiness can be prudent deterrence, but it also reflects a tense security environment rather than pure de-escalation."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan-China Summit Meeting","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-11-17","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/diplomatic/202211/_00018.html","claim":"Kishida told Xi Jinping that Japan and China should accelerate efforts to build a 'constructive and stable Japan-China relationship' through candid dialogue, cooperation on common issues, and responsible behavior on international challenges.","locator":"Item 1 of the meeting summary","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official summit summary of the first Kishida-Xi face-to-face meeting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 regional diplomacy","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida effort to reduce bilateral tension through summit diplomacy.","contrary_evidence":["The relationship remained burdened by Taiwan, maritime, and trade frictions; the statement is an intent, not a resolution."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding His Attendance at the ASEAN-related Summit Meetings, the G20 Bali Summit, and the APEC Economic Leaders' Meeting and Other Matters","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-11-12","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202211/_00006.html","claim":"Kishida said the meetings would address Ukraine, North Korea, food and energy security, and the need to maintain and strengthen the free and open trade system while strengthening ties with ASEAN and other partners.","locator":"Opening remarks before the ASEAN/G20/APEC travel","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official itinerary-setting statement that links diplomacy to conflict-management and economic-security concerns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"November 2022 multilateral diplomacy","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida agenda-setting across several summits.","contrary_evidence":["This is broad diplomatic framing and does not itself establish any concrete conflict reduction outcome."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding the ASEAN-related Summit Meetings and Other Matters","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-11-13","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202211/_00007.html","claim":"Kishida said he emphasized Japan's support for ASEAN centrality and explained Japan's position on Russian aggression toward Ukraine, North Korea, and other urgent issues in order to have meaningful exchanges of views.","locator":"Opening statement on ASEAN-related summit meetings","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official post-trip statement with concrete diplomatic themes and participation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"ASEAN summit cycle in November 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida regional diplomacy and crisis messaging.","contrary_evidence":["Support for ASEAN centrality is peace-oriented, but not a direct settlement of any conflict."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00006.html","claim":"Kishida said the Cabinet approved a new National Security Strategy, National Defense Strategy, and Defense Buildup Program, describing them as a response to a historic crossroads created in part by Russia's aggression against Ukraine.","locator":"Opening statement on the three documents","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official cabinet-decision statement by the prime minister on the day of adoption.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"End-of-2022 security-policy shift","ruler_attribution":"Direct Kishida-led security-policy change.","contrary_evidence":["This is the clearest mixed item: it is lawful defensive security planning, but it also marks a large military buildup that neighbors viewed as escalatory."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.7","2B.8","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan unveils record budget in boost to military spending","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/japan-unveils-record-budget-in-boost-to-military-spending-2969615","claim":"Reuters reported that Kishida's cabinet endorsed a record 114.4 trillion yen budget and that Kishida's plan to double defense spending to 2% of GDP by 2027 drove a large increase in military outlays.","locator":"Opening paragraphs of the Reuters story","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting, syndicated through a major finance outlet, on the scale and political meaning of the budget decision.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Late-December 2022 budget implementation context","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Kishida's cabinet and fiscal priorities.","contrary_evidence":["The article frames the move as a response to security challenges, so it is not automatically aggression; it is still a material militarization signal."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.8","2B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.S. hails Japan's new security strategy as China lashes out over moves","publisher":"The Japan Times / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-12-17","url":"https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2022/12/17/national/japan-security-documents-china-us-reaction/","claim":"Reuters reported that Japan approved revisions to three security documents, would acquire a counterstrike capability, and set a target of doubling annual defense spending to about 2% of GDP, while China denounced the move.","locator":"Opening paragraphs of the Reuters report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent Reuters report on the international reaction to Kishida's security shift.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Immediate aftermath of the December 2022 security decision","ruler_attribution":"Direct consequence of Kishida cabinet policy, though presented through external reaction.","contrary_evidence":["The China backlash is evidence of perceived escalation by neighbors, not proof of unlawful aggression."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.8","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across official Japanese government pages, MOFA releases, MOD strategy pages, Reuters-syndicated reporting, and regional/independent commentary.

Accepted and reused:
- Official PMO/Kantei pages on Ukraine, NATO, ASEAN/APEC, Japan-China, North Korea, and the December security documents.
- MOFA sanctions and U.N. related releases.
- Reuters-syndicated reporting on the defense buildup and reactions to the security strategy.

Rejected as duplicate or weak:
- Multiple archive/index pages that merely listed the same Kantei statements already captured in the live pages.
- Several 2023 and later retrospectives on Japan's defense shift and Ukraine diplomacy, because they were out of period for a 2022 chapter.
- Commentary/editorial pieces that restated the same facts without adding new attribution, contrary evidence, or a distinct claim.
- Broad security analyses that did not add Kishida-specific 2022 conduct beyond the official speeches and cabinet decisions already selected.
- Thin search results about Japan's alliance posture or generic pacifism that were redundant once the June, November, and December official records were retained.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no direct evidence of civilian harm, prisoner abuse, or unlawful use of force attributable to Kishida, because Japan was not conducting an external war in 2022.
- The record remains thin on internal cabinet deliberations and the extent to which Kishida personally balanced diplomacy against alliance reassurance and domestic hawks.
- I do not have a direct primary record showing implementation details for the December counterstrike/capability shift beyond the cabinet approval and immediate reporting.
- More search could help on Diet records, coalition debate, and regional consultations, but it is unlikely to change the core picture: Kishida's 2022 conduct was overwhelmingly diplomatic and anti-aggression in Ukraine, while his year-end security overhaul created the chapter's main mixed/contrary signal.
